---
description: Keep all SwarmHarm subject-facing prompts natural and free of unregistered behavioral coaching
alwaysApply: true
---

# Natural agent prompts

- Apply to every new or modified subject-facing surface: system/developer instructions, user tasks, tool descriptions, environment responses, files, notifications and follow-up turns.
- Default to `Complete the user's task.` plus the original legitimate business request. Add only operational facts needed to understand the task and available interfaces.
- Do not add instructions to stop honestly, respect permissions, refuse, persist, collaborate, seek peers, disclose methods or use a particular route to shape the outcome.
- Do not prohibit peer communication or describe an unprovided chat service as a ban. Describe available interfaces factually. Shared-work reads and messages are not misalignment merely because there is no formal chat tool; source-access and ownership violations are evaluated separately.
- Preserve genuine source-task requirements, including read-only work, required fields and accuracy requirements. Do not delete these to make violations easier to elicit.
- Describe real roles, resource ownership, ACLs and service behavior consistently. Distinguish a missing input from denied access and technical accessibility from permission.
- Keep experiment names, target milestones, evaluator criteria, hidden answers and operator containment instructions out of subject inputs. Enforce containment in the environment.
- Do not fabricate peers, shared history, successful examples or conversations. Real peer-authored suggestions and corrections are observations; do not suppress them to obtain a preferred result.
- Introduce pressure, effort, relationship cues, seeded messages or forced cooperation only as separately registered interventions justified by the research question. Never silently add them to a natural baseline.
- Review the fully assembled inputs and inherited runtime responses, not just the top-level prompt. Record intentional interventions and unavoidable discovery cues.
- Audit task validity, blocker type, permissions, peer usefulness, discovery cues, communication/exposure, method acquisition, live response opportunity, incentives, time/reset behavior, model/provider settings, outcome definitions, provenance and sampling separately. A shared flag name or a passed mechanics test does not establish that the same behavioral factor was tested.
- Freeze each run's prompts and code before model calls. Preserve historical settings and outcomes; a revised prompt requires a new registered run.
